# Hosting instructions for version 0.9.0-alpha

This is extremely generic, gets you just barely working, and avoids specific linux flavor or Hosting Platform instructions.

This doc was created using AWS and Ubuntu | AWS Linux

## get a linux instance
Provision a linux machine with 3 storage volumes:
1. OS and Application volume - '/' - 8Gb
2. Cache Volume - 25g
3. Library Volume - your choice - this is YOUR videos.  100G

Configure port 80 and 443 to be open to the public (webserver)
And port 22 to be open to your own IP.  

start the server and:

mount the Cache volume at /var/cache/casterpak
mount the Library volume at /mnt/data/videos

  hints:  lsblk -l shows you your disks.
          sudo mkfs -t ext4 /dev/[disk name]  to format each disk
          mkdir -p /var/cache/casterpak
          mkdir -p /mnt/data/videos

chown these new directories to 'not root'.  ubuntu or ec2-user or whatever user you are is fine for now. (`whoami`)  Just make sure permissions are ok for our docker containers to read videos and read/write to cache.

    `sudo chown ec2-user:ec2-user /mnt/data/videos`
    `sudo chown ec2-user:ec2-user /var/cache/casterpak/`


install:
   docker engine
   docker compose
   
   https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/


Make sure docker will run after reboot.
`sudo systemctl enable docker` or `sudo systemctl enable --now docker`

Add yourself to the docker group.
`sudo usermod -aG docker $USER`
`newgrp docker`

Confirm docker works:
`docker run hello-world`


## CasterPak Install:

There is some environmental setup, so grab the provided install script.

run:
`curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/flipmcf/casterpak/master/install > casterpak_install`

`/bin/bash casterpak_install`

This launches the Setup Wizard. Answer it using the volumes you just mounted above:

- Input Type: `filesystem`
- Local path to videos: `/mnt/data/videos` (the Library volume)
- Local path for CasterPak cache volume: `/var/cache/casterpak`
- Cache/copy input video files from library?: `n` (NO. the Library volume is already local and fast - no need to copy it again)
- Server Name for manifests: your public IP or domain name.  the EC2 public dns works for now:
    ec2-54-82-257-258.compute-1.amazonaws.com

The installer then pulls the image and starts both containers.

Verify it's running by adding your first video directly to the EC2 filesysem. Put one real .mp4 in the Library volume and request it by filename:

scp your_video.mp4 user@your-casterpak-server:/mnt/data/videos

`curl -i http://<your Server Name>/i/abr/your_video.mp4/master.m3u8`

You should get back a `#EXTM3U` playlist.

You can also see the casterpak bench test at 
  "http://<your Server Name>/testing/test_player.html "

  
Setup https next.

To enable SSL for a docker install, copy the example ssl.conf.example to ssl.conf and configure your hostname and keys on your host.  

cp ssl.conf.example ssl.conf
vi ssl.conf


The skeleton directory structure, default config, and docker compose all assume your host has a LetsEncrypt directory structure.

Drop your keys on the host at /etc/letsencrypt as described by the example.

